      "text_raw": "A first-order low-pass filter\nis an integrator with a resis-\ntor across the capacitor.\nIt is also called a lossy\nintegrator.\nAt lower and lower fre-\nquencies, the gain is now set\nby the ratio of the two\nresistors.\nAt the pole frequency, or\nthe bandwidth, the phase\nshift is exactly halfway\nbetween zero and 90°, which\nis 45°.\nNote that a first-order\ndecreasing characteristic,\nwhich is called a pole,\nalways shows a slope of\n−20 dB/decade and −90°\nphase shift.",
      "text": "A first-order low-pass filter is an integrator with a resistor across the capacitor. It is also called a lossy integrator. At lower and lower frequencies, the gain is now set by the ratio of the two resistors. At the pole frequency, or the bandwidth, the phase shift is exactly halfway between zero and 90°, which is 45°. Note that a first-order decreasing characteristic, which is called a pole, always shows a slope of −20 dB/decade and −90° phase shift."
    }
